# Source distribution contents — blackwattle.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THIS IS NOT A ZERO-TRUST PACKAGE. Every third-party import in the shipped
# modules is lazy/optional (in-function import or try/except fallback), but the
# installed surface is third-party-coupled and the user audits it (CLAUDE.md §7.4).
#
# Hard dependencies: stdlib + `wattleflow-workflow` (which brings `wattleflow`).
# Everything else — cryptography included — is an OPTIONAL extra in
# pyproject.toml, so a bare `pip install blackwattle` pulls nothing unvetted.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `global-exclude *` below drops everything first, so this file is the single
# control point: a package that `packages.find` declares but this file omits
# installs EMPTY (DR-WFL-006, DR-WFL-015 / SEC-03 K12). Keep the two in step.
#
# INVARIANT: one `recursive-include` here per entry in
# `[tool.setuptools.packages.find] include` in pyproject.toml — fifteen, in the
# same order. Both lists are whole-subtree, so adding a specialization under an
# existing package needs no edit in either file; adding a new top-level package
# needs an edit in BOTH.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Names shared with wattleflow-workflow — `constants`, `decorators`, `enums`,
# `helpers`, `mappers` — are PEP 420 namespaces: no `__init__.py` in either tree,
# each tree holds only its own modules (DR-WFL-017). Measured 2026-09-08: the two
# trees have no module name in common under any of the five, so nothing foreign
# can be swept in and a whole-package rule is safe here.
#
# That measurement is why `helpers` is a single recursive rule rather than the
# per-module list this file used to carry. The list was not safer — it was one
# more place to forget: it had already lost `helpers/image_security.py` and
# `helpers/metrics.py`, and still named `helpers/image/`, a directory that does
# not exist. A rule that tracks the tree cannot drift from it.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The core, concrete, managers, orchestrators and schedulers layers are reachable
# here only through dev symlinks and must never be re-shipped from this sdist.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

global-exclude *

# --- Packages owned entirely by this distribution ---
# One line per pyproject `include` entry, same order.
recursive-include src/wattleflow/blackboards *.py
recursive-include src/wattleflow/connections *.py
recursive-include src/wattleflow/constants *.py
recursive-include src/wattleflow/decorators *.py
recursive-include src/wattleflow/documents *.py
recursive-include src/wattleflow/drivers *.py
recursive-include src/wattleflow/enums *.py
recursive-include src/wattleflow/helpers *.py
recursive-include src/wattleflow/mappers *.py
recursive-include src/wattleflow/metrics *.py
recursive-include src/wattleflow/oscal *.py
recursive-include src/wattleflow/pipelines *.py
recursive-include src/wattleflow/processors *.py
recursive-include src/wattleflow/repositories *.py
recursive-include src/wattleflow/strategies *.py

# --- Never ship layers that belong to another distribution ---
# Defensive: these are absent from a clean checkout but reappear as dev symlinks,
# and must not be swept into this sdist when they do. `global-exclude *` already
# covers it; these state the boundary where a reader looks for it.
prune src/wattleflow/core
prune src/wattleflow/concrete

# --- Modules held back from release ---
# An sdist is built from the WORKING TREE, not from git: a module that is
# present on disk but untracked still ships unless a rule drops it. These files
# are untracked by `.gitignore` and must stay out of the distribution too — the
# pattern is kept identical to the one in `.gitignore` so the two cannot drift.
# Must follow the `recursive-include` lines above: later rules win.
global-exclude *enti*.py

# --- Documentation lives in its own repository ---
# `documentation` is a symlink to the separate, local-only documentation
# repository (CLAUDE.md §8); it is no longer tracked here, but it still resolves
# in a developer tree, which is exactly when this rule matters. Pruning keeps
# another repository's content — and its C-snapshots — out of this distribution
# (NFRQ-SEC-02, minimal surface). `Makefile`, likewise a dev symlink and no
# longer tracked, needs no rule: `global-exclude *` never included it.
prune documentation

# --- Package data: vendored OSCAL catalogues, read at runtime by the loaders ---
# `include-package-data = false` means this line fills the SDIST only; the WHEEL
# needs the matching `[tool.setuptools.package-data]` entry in pyproject.toml.
# Two files, one fact — change them together.
recursive-include src/wattleflow/oscal/resources *.json

# --- Repository metadata ---
include README.md LICENSE pyproject.toml requirements-dev.txt
